{
  "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
  "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nGREGORY G. RAPAWY, ESQ., Washington, D.C.;\non behalf of the Petitioners.\nMALCOLM L. STEWART, Deputy Solicitor General,\nDepartment of Justice, Washington, D.C.;\non behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "SEC disgorgement orders compel a payment to the Treasury as a consequence for violation of a public law. An order like that is a penalty, as this Court's unanimous decision in Kokesh makes clear."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Rapawy, you started out by saying Kokesh labeled this a penalty and equity doesn't enforce penalties and that's it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RAPAWY: Justice Ginsburg, I certainly agree that this Court reserved this question in Kokesh in Footnote 3. And my argument is not that the holding of that case resolves this case but that the reasoning of that case can't effectively been -- be distinguished from this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Would it be -- I thought there were efforts to get the money to the investors. It doesn't require the money to be paid into the Treasury. If the SEC can locate the investors and get the money back to them, the SEC says that's what it would do."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. RAPAWY",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They -- they do that in some cases, Your Honor. They do not do it in all cases. It is difficult from the public materials to determine how often they do it and how much money they do give back to investors."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: On the --on the question of giving money back to the investors, I think Justice Ginsburg raised the issue about maybe you can't find them, they're not identifiable, there are too many of them. How -- what -- what do you think that if -- if we -- if we said, you know, it's an equitable principle that the money should go back to the investors if possible, what does that mean exactly that the SEC has to do?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. RAPAWY",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would say that if you were to take that position and disagree with my primary argument, it would -- then the --the rule should be, if you're giving the money back to the investors, then you can take it and not otherwise, because if you're not giving it back to the investors, then it's just a punishment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, you know, make -- make good-faith efforts; make, you know, diligent efforts. What -- whatever words you want to use."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. RAPAWY",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0011",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I mean, Your Honor could certainly write that a decision --in a decision. I don't think it would be sufficient guidance or sufficient compulsion to the agency to ensure that this was used for compensatory purposes and not for punitory --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: And they come from equity and traditional principles of equity, right? I mean, they're drawn from that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: And may I ask you about your net profits rule, a similar kind of question? I mean, what does the SEC, in your view, have to deduct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0012",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF MALCOLM L. STEWART ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: But your -- your position is, if I understand it correctly, follow whatever the common law rule was with respect to calculating net profits, return it to investors, but that you're also a victim and so that you -- you could take the money ahead of investors, that you can keep the leftover amounts? What -- what is your position with respect to that broader question of who gets the money? Why is it the Treasury? It's not the SEC getting the money."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. STEWART",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0014",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Let me say three or four things in -- in response to that. The first is that, as an empirical matter, the SEC tries to return the money to investors when it can, and we're largely successful in doing that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. STEWART",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0015",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I mean, sometimes it is done and sometimes it is not done. Sometimes the reason that it is not done is, as I was saying with respect to the FCPA, there just is no obvious universe of investors."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Would the government have any difficulty with a rule that the money should be returned to investors where feasible?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. STEWART",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0016",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would say if -- if that is couched as a general equitable principle; that is, the court is sitting as a court of equity, there would be nothing wrong with a district judge in an individual case saying unless you can persuade me that it is infeasible to return this money to investors, I am going to order that that be done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. STEWART",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0001-0017",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I -- I wouldn't have a problem with that. I mean, I don't know that it is kind of in accordance with usual principles for the Court to announce that sort of instruction, but it would be consistent with the SEC's practice."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gregory G. Rapawy)",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "he characterized the\ngovernment as having conceded that our\ndisgorgement is a substantial departure from\nhistorical norms. And that -- that's not really\nwhat we said."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gregory G. Rapawy)",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "started to become prevalent, courts had to --to\ngrapple with questions about how do legal\nprinciples that were developed in the context of\nprivate suits map onto government enforcement\nactions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gregory G. Rapawy)",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0002-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And as of 1850, that was an open\nquestion. By the time that Congress enacted\nSection 21(d)(5) in 2002, that question had\nreally been resolved, because this Court in\nPorter and Mitchell had said the federal courts'\nequitable powers are at their height when the\npublic interest is involved."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What is your answer\n-- what is your response to the argument, if I\nhave it right, that in equity, the closest thing\nis restitution, and in Great-West, the majority\nsaid: Well, restitution was an equitable remedy\nwhen it was a case in equity, but it was a legal\nremedy when it was a case in law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gregory G. Rapawy)",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Rapawy's argument with respect to Livingston\nand the patent cases, I mean, before the Court\nhad specific statutory authority to do so, in\ncases like Livingston, the Court held that a\ndefendant's profits were the --were an\nappropriate element of relief in a patent\ninfringement suit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gregory G. Rapawy)",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-62ee51fc719c-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think its practical function has\nbeen to compel payments to the Treasury. There\nis no historical precedent for that. I would\ncite to the Court's Gabelli case, in which the\nCourt found that there was no precedent for\napplying the equitable doctrine of the discovery\nrule to -- to cases by the government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1501",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "62ee51fc719c4d47b1aae27464aa6f36488a81fd72bcec005070df246e3520e9",
      "evidence_id": "unavailable-62ee51fc719c-57",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "unavailable",
      "text": "OCR unavailable"
    },
